---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const description =
  'The terms for using Pixel Port: beta software provided as-is, honest compatibility labels, your games stay yours, no anti-cheat circumvention.';
---

<Base title="Terms of Use" description={description}>
  <article class="wrap max-w-3xl py-16 sm:py-20">
    <nav class="text-sm text-muted">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-fg">Home</a> <span class="px-1">/</span> Terms
    </nav>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-balance text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
      Terms of Use
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">Last updated: 2026-07-01</p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted">
      Plain-language terms for using Pixel Port. We have kept these short and honest; using Pixel
      Port means you agree to them.
    </p>

    <div class="prose prose-invert mt-12 max-w-none prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-accent-2">
      <h2>Beta software, provided as-is</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Port is beta software. It is provided as-is, without warranties of any kind, and we
        cannot guarantee it will be error-free or that every game will run. We work hard to make it
        reliable, and we fix things fast, but you use it at your own risk.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest compatibility, no promises</h2>
      <p>
        Game compatibility varies, and we label it honestly: <strong>verified</strong> means a human
        confirmed the game end-to-end on a real Mac; <strong>playable</strong> means it is graded from
        real launches or trusted reports but not yet hand-verified; <strong>untested</strong> and
        <strong>unsupported</strong> mean exactly what they say. A tier is our best current knowledge,
        not a guarantee that a game will run on your specific machine.
      </p>

      <h2>Your games stay yours</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Port does not give you games. You must own the games you play; Pixel Port installs them
        through your own Steam account, with your own licenses. You are responsible for complying
        with the terms of the stores and games you use.
      </p>

      <h2>No anti-cheat circumvention</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Port does not bypass, disable, or circumvent anti-cheat systems, and you may not use it
        to attempt that. Games whose anti-cheat cannot run on macOS are labeled unsupported, and we
        tell you plainly why.
      </p>

      <h2>Pricing</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Port is free to use during the beta. Pricing may be introduced later, with notice: beta
        users will be told clearly before anything is ever charged. Nothing will silently start
        costing money.
      </p>

      <h2>Fair use</h2>
      <p>
        Do not abuse the service: no attempts to disrupt or reverse-engineer our backend, scrape it at
        scale, submit falsified compatibility data, or use Pixel Port for anything unlawful.
      </p>

      <h2>Your data</h2>
      <p>
        How we handle data, including anonymous-by-default telemetry, is covered in the
        <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes and termination</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms as Pixel Port evolves; the date above will always reflect the
        current version, and meaningful changes will be called out. We may suspend access that
        violates these terms. You can stop using Pixel Port at any time, and you can request deletion
        of your data via support.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about these terms: reach us through the support channel listed on
        <a href="/">pixelport.gg</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
